I remember-- I'll never forget that, as we were going away, they were taking us, and my cousin came along. My cousin came along because she was afraid they'll catch her someplace else and she'll be alone. This way, she will be with us.

We turned our heads and we saw my two little cousins. [CRYING] And my uncle was saying goodbye to us. [CRYING] Not knowing-- we knew that we are not going to see them anymore. And whenever I close my eyes, I still can see them. [CRYING]

Only, only the one who lived through this can truly understand. You feel it in their heart what it is to be separated, to lose loved ones, not to see them anymore. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I'm the one who truly lost a lot. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

We arrived in camp. The camp, Karolówka, was situated [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] in between a lot of land. This land belonged once to I think Polish people. And we were taken to a labor camp. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] When we came there, there were already people there. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

My sister, as I said, was lucky that she was tall so she could pass for a older girl. We were all three together. We found ourselves a corner where to sleep. We did not have any money. And we were told what we are going to do. We have to work, sunrise, sunset. In the morning again.

You get a slice of bread, black coffee. Lunchtime, we could hardly wait it should be lunchtime already. Because we used to go back to the barracks and get the food. It was potato soup. In the potato soup, you could find a lot of flies.

My brother, or too, I was suffering, and lived through so much, but to eat flies, I just couldn't. So my brother used to fetch this soup and take out the flies before he gave me. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] But my brother says to me, at least we are still alive. In our heart, we were buying time.

The days that we could breathe, the days that we were alive, the days that we worked, we were buying-- we were alive. We were buying time. We always hoped. We were young people. And my parents said, do not lose hope.

INT: Did anybody in your family, or the people among whom you were living, have any idea about the progress of the war--

RK: No.

INT: --and what was happening?

RK: No, no, we did not know nothing. If sometimes somebody will mention something, we will not answer or ask. We never knew what the other person feels or wants us to know. So we just kept quiet. You never knew who the enemy or your friend is, so you never talked. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We worked there on the fields again. And we were planting [NON-ENGLISH]. [NON-ENGLISH] is a-- I think they call it [NON-ENGLISH] bush that they-- excuse me. They told us that this is something when you cut it some milk, like milk was coming out. And this they used to take out from the grounds, pack it, and send it to Germany. In Germany, they made tires out of it. And be careful, do not cut out, because you'll be beaten to hell. So we watched.

We worked planting potatoes. And even in the rain, or, or-- rain, we, we had to work. Not one word we did something else. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] When, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] when the things were ripe to take out, like potatoes were grown, the, the corns were OK, the wheat was growing, we used to pick up the-- take together potatoes in sacks and carry.

Till today I have a broken spine from that. But I somehow managed. Instead of a whole sack, till I came to my destiny, as I told you before I always carried a piece of glass with me. I cut through the threads that the sack was sewn. Until I came to my destiny, a lot of potatoes were missing. That was my salvation.

There was nothing to it to do. We worked very hard. And we prayed to God the, the war should end.

One night as we were sleeping, we heard a commotion outside the barrack in Karolówka. We didn't know what was happening. Russian partisans came to us. We were shocked.

INT: Into the camp?

RK: Into the camp.

INT: What happened to the German guards there?

RK: They were asleep or sometimes they-- we-- in that camp, we did not have Russian guards, we had Ukrainian guards. Or they went drinking, God knows what. Knocked on the door. We got scared. We got up and opened the door. We had to open. Everybody was afraid. We had to open whoever it is. I took a look and here, Russian soldiers.

INT: In Russian army uniform.

RK: In Russian uniform, with the Russian star, with the rifle. Whatever, of course, I don't know how to name it. And he looks at us and I look at him. And we look at him. And we-- I speak to him in Russian, what are you doing here?

And he says [RUSSIAN], which he means, hello, we are Russian partisans, and who are you? We are Jewish people. We are in a camp. This is a labor camp. How did you get here?

They were going to a forest. From the forest they came out to-- on the fields. And then they saw barracks. They didn't know this was a camp. And they came in. So I told them you're lucky that nobody is here at the moment.

My brother was very much against that I should go out, but I did. I went out. A young fellow-- I asked him, how old are you? 18. And we started to exchange a few words. And he says to us, keep it up, the war will end and we'll win it.

And he asked us-- everybody came out of from the camp-- asked us how to get to a certain place. They were going deeper into the territory towards Germany. I said, why are you going there? We're going undermine trains, sabotaging. So we told them be very careful, because the people here don't like the Russians. The deeper you go, the more dangerous it is.

He said, but we have an order to do it and we can't help it. They, they asked us what can we do for you? We'll burn the place. We said, no, you'll burn the place, they'll kill us. I spoke fluently Russian. My brother didn't come out. He was inside. I didn't let him.

Shall I burn the wheat? As I told you, when you take the, the straw that has the wheat-- I don't know how to say it-- together, they create such a big whatever it is, but together. So he says, we are going to burn it. I said, please do not do anything. We begged him, not only I, but other ones too.

They went into the director of the camp and stole a pair of boots. We begged him not to do anything there too, because then he'll blame us. Nevertheless, he needed a pair of boots and he stole the boots. I remember, because we had-- I didn't have any money, but the Jewish people had to buy boots.

They wanted to go to [NON-ENGLISH] forest. [NON-ENGLISH] is a name of a, of a city. And they have forests, very, very famous. It means famous, when you get in, and you don't know how to get out, you can die there. And from there, they had a map. They have to cut through Ukraine and go towards Germany. And they were asking where the trains are. Frankly, honestly, we told them, we don't know.

But there was a fellow among us. He says, I'll take you there and I'll show you. Maybe some people don't understand it, but just to see somebody who can give us freedom means a lot. [CRYING] As he took the, the partisans-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] as he took the partisans and went out from the camp-- I don't know how far he was-- here we hear dogs barking, noise-- the Gestapo, the Germans are after him, after the-- after them.

Lucky our doors were closed. Lights we didn't have in camp. And when we heard a noise, everybody lied down. And somebody looked through the window and says, Germans. We pretended we slept. Knocked in the door and asked, did you see a group of Russian partisans here, because the dogs with the smell brought them. And here, I was outside. I truly was frightened.

And we said, no, no, we didn't see nobody. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] We had words that they were passing by through. We were after them and we traced them here. Maybe they were outside, maybe. But we are-- our door's closed. We work here. We are in-- this is a labor camp.

But here in our heart we were terrified because this young fellow took the partisans out and we were afraid that he's going to come and meet him. He'll see them. He was very smart too. He heard a commotion, so he hid. When the Germans went away he came into the barracks, the fellow. We were so happy to see him. Because i they would have caught him, we were all been lost. This is one incident.

We used to get up in the morning and work from sunrise to sunset. Again, the same food was given to us-- coffee, bread, and the soup. After we did our work, we were kind of frightened because we were afraid of the Germans are not going to use us anymore. And by not using us, it means that they're going to liquidate us.

But another camp needed people. So a certain group of people was transferred to another camp. And I was taken to Laszkowce-- Ulaszkowce. I pronounce it Ulaszkowce-- Laszkowce. A group from us was transferred there. We came to that camp because the previous camp-- the people that were previously in camp were all killed. And they needed help.

We didn't know what was going on. This was already in 1943. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] We didn't know that the Germans are retreating. They needed people to take the-- from the fields, potatoes from the fields, vegetables, cows. Everything they were taking. They needed people to work.

And we worked there. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We had Germans on, on-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] camp, in our camp. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They didn't bother us. They were happy that we were-- those were plain, simple soldiers. They were very happy that we were helping them. The more we worked, easier it was for them because they didn't have to do. They just supervised it. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

But the problem was that we were afraid for the Ukrainians. Next to that camp was a grave-- a grave. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And when the Germans came in, the whole town, Ulaszkowce, was killed. My friend lived through that time. A Ukrainian fellow saved her with her husband, but nobody else survived. She made a statement too, the same thing that I'm doing, but she never mentioned anything. I'm saying it.

When I walked out, the gate with right in front of us. We worked there, the same thing, long hours, snow or rain, there was no excuse. If you were terribly sick, you had to hide in the barracks that nobody should know that you are not going to work. Otherwise, you'll be punished.

INT: At any of the camps that you stayed in, were there any of these appells that they had in any of the camps, where you had to stand out in the rain and--

RK: Oh, yeah.

INT: --be counted?

RK: Oh, yes, oh, yes, sure, yes.

INT: Then what would happen if the person was sick, and tried to hide, and didn't show up for the appell?

RK: Well, they'll walk into the barracks and see he's sick, he truly can't. But you'll be surprised. God gives a-- God gave us strength. I used to be a very sick girl, always a sore throat when I was a kid, I couldn't eat. I was strong. I was very strong. I can-- sometimes I close my eyes and think. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

But that director of the camp had some Jewish people working for him in his house. I forgot his name and I should have remembered. While he was away, the Jewish people used to put on the radio and find out what's going on in the world. My God, one came running. He says, the, the Russian-- the Russians are dancing. Kharkov is being taken. We didn't make a big issue, because we didn't believe him any way, it's impossible. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

[SIGHS]

Till-- [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] while we were working there, packing all these things for the Germans to take, we used to steal food. We steal-- what did we steal? Potatoes. What can you steal? You're afraid to take something else-- and maybe beans too. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] At least we could cook outside or, again, on three rocks, you can cook anything you want to.

And things were kind of quiet. We worked hard. We said to ourselves like this. We had elderly people, religious people. We always asked their-- for suggestion, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the older people. What do you think? The war's going to finish? What do you think? They need us? Do you think they'll liquidate us?

And he says, kids, people, as long as the Germans need us, nobody's going to touch us. Because they need our work, he said. So we were happy to hear that. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

As the Germans were losing the war, things were getting much harder for us. But it-- as hard as they were, there's nothing anybody could do about it. We just had to be stronger. If they wanted us to work at night, we worked at night.

If they wanted to shovel the snow, we went to shovel the snow in the rags. If they wanted to-- we should go in the rain, in snow, shovel the roads because their tanks should be able to pass, we went. You couldn't say no. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But as long as nobody was killed, we were happy.

One day a German soldier came into our barrack, approached my brother. Because my brother was like the eldest. Not the eldest, he was like-- he spoke fluently German. And whenever he had to talk to the Germans, we sent him.

A German came in and he says, we are making a ball, who can play-- are you-- any one among you that plays [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] piano, violin. And whoever will play, we'll give him a bread, we'll give him food, we'll reward him. But we need somebody. We, we don't have anybody-- nobody, anybody. And here, the people are coming from Chortkiv.

You know who it was? The Gestapo, big shots. The directors from the camp wife-- her sweetheart was-- she had a sweetheart because he was a old man and she was younger, a fat lady-- her boyfriend, her sweetheart, was a Gestapo man. He, he was there too.

So my brother says, I play. When he said that, I walked over and I, and I pinched him. And I said in Polish, remember what my parents said, play dumb. He says, I don't care one way or another. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It came to a time-- he was my inspiration, but it came to times that you just didn't want to face anymore the day. You just didn't want to live. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

But we had to go on. What could you do? Go commit suicide? That's-- that was not in our game. We were fighting to survive.

And my brother went. I was a nervous wreck. They were all dressed up, the Germans, beautifully. It's a ball. My poor brother went to [INAUDIBLE] jacket. I think he had some kind of shoes that he made for himself, wooden-- he had found a skin from a calf. And he cleaned it, treated it. And somebody gave him-- cut for him wooden soles. And he attached it and made beautiful shoes. At least his foot was not in the mud.

And I said, take a look how we look and how they look. He says, don't worry, I'll be back. I'll never forget how I looked after him. He says, I'll be back. And the German was very happy that they found somebody that plays.

My brother went to play. I didn't sleep. We were sitting and waiting for him. He, he was brilliant in whatever he touched, not because he's my brother, but that is the truth.

As he was playing, a German walked over to him. And he says to my brother, where did you learn to play? Self-taught. Where did you learn to speak so beautiful German? Self-taught, my father was in the Austrian army, the same story, so we spoke German.

I have a secret to tell you. Tuesday they're going to-- they're going to liquidate the whole camp. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] My brother didn't answer him because he was scared to say anything. He finished playing. The Germans gave him a bread. The Germans gave him food. And he came back to the camp. When I saw him, I thought that-- I thought that God came from, from, from heaven.

He says, Rachel, I have to tell you something. I said, what do you have to tell me? I was playing and the German came over to me, and this what he said, that Tuesday they're going to liquidate all the Jewish people. And what-- was he sober? Was he drinking? So he says, he was very drunk.

So I said, you know what? We have to tell the eldest. We walked over to the, the people and we told him that. Ah, he was drinking. He was drunk. He didn't know what he was talking about. And we didn't pay any attention to it. They all said this. So I thought they were smarter than I. And this what we listened to. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

[SIGHS]

We decided not to make a big issue out of it and life was going on. But my sister a few days before showed me a place, an opening in a gate. She ripped off-- it's not a gate-- two pieces of wood. How do you call this-- not a gate, but all around the house when you put, how do you call that?

INT: A fence?

RK: A fence, a fence made from wood, from wood. She ripped off two pieces and she said, Rachel, this is someday going to save my life. And remember. And if you, and if you were-- have to use it, use it.

INT: OK, we're now pause so we can change the tapes.

RK: Who's here?

INT: That's your husband.INT: This is tape number nine in the interview with Mrs. Rachel Kriegel. In the last tape, at the end, you were telling us about the plans to liquidate the ghetto. And your sister showing you the two--

RK: The camp.

INT: --the two--

RK: Liquidate the camp.

INT: Right, liquidating the camp, I'm sorry. And your sister were showing you the two loose boards in the-- in the fence there. Would you want to continue with your story now?

RK: Yes. And I, I-- and, and she says to me, don't touch it. Nobody should see it. Because we can be punished. And that was the end of it. We came in the evening. Everybody came from work.

Naturally, what did we eat for dinner? Black coffee and, and a piece of bread. Whoever from our people had a little money, he could buy something. How did he buy?

The people-- the Ukrainian people, Polish people-- were very eager to sell things to the Jewish people. Because they got paid very well for-- excuse me. For a few pounds potatoes, they could obtain God knows what.

And we went to sleep, not thinking about that Tuesday is coming, not thinking about that they're going to liquidate the camp. Just exhausted, tired, worn out, we went to sleep.

Early in the morning, it was still dark outside. We heard the bell ringing. We got up, we didn't know where we are. When you sleep, you get up frightened. And my brother says to me, oh, they're calling us to work. They're calling us to appell, to be counted, and then to send us to work.

But so early? It's dark. We don't-- it's dark. Never happened before. It never came to our mind that something is going to happen. He went to the-- to the leader-- he was German-- of the camp to talk to him. Because he spoke English fluently.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

He never came back.

INT: You mean German?

RK: I mean German fluent. Never came back. We didn't pay any attention, because he just walked away. And here the people were finishing the coffee and running to work.

While he was walking to the German, the Germans caught him, or the Ukrainian police, took him and put him in the stable, and gave a Ukrainian man to watch him, and said, don't let him out. In meantime, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] other-- from other camps, a lot of workers were sent into our camp.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

They told them that in Lisowce, the Germans are packing things, and they are taking away, and they need help. So every camp has a responsibility to help the Germans. And this were they're sending them. Because it was-- then it was a little bit suspicious. Why are they sending some people there? We had enough help there. Who knew? Who was thinking of anything?

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

As, as the doors from-- this camp was surrounded with a wall in Lisowce. As the gates open, the people came in. The gates closed. The Germans start shooting. Surrounded all the people.

I remained in the barracks that time, I and my cousin, and a friend of mine, [? Nusha-- ?] her sister was sick. We hid her under the bed. And, and I don't remember anybody else. We heard shooting. We still didn't believe it, that, that something has happened.

As I ran to the door to, to look, a policemen-- Ukrainian policeman-- saw me at the door. From far away, he was running. He was shooting, but he didn't get me. And I ask him, oh, my brother! Oh, my sister! Oh, my brother! Oh, my sister! As I was-- I think I, I broke down. I didn't know what's going on.

My friend pushed her sister out of the window. My cousin jumped out through the window. But she saw me kind of hysterical, came over, grabbed me by my back, and, and pried the window open more, and gave me a shovel to the window, that I rolled down-- the hill, because the camp was on a hill-- and fell into the water.

I was soaking wet. This was November 30th. I had nothing on my feet, not even a piece of schmatte rag. Nothing. Because I was inside. We were supposed to cook potatoes, and the potatoes were very dirty. So we-- I-- they took-- I was washing them.

From there, we ran. We ran from the-- we ran through the water. I couldn't walk. But from the trees, some kind of a stickers stuck into my feet. I said, I can't run, you run. They dragged me, but they didn't leave me. And we came to a forest. Through the fields, we came to a forest. We came to the forest--

INT: Excuse me for interrupting. But to understand this clearly-- did you use those two boards that you-- loose boards-- that your sister--

RK: No.

INT: --showed you to escape?

RK: No, no no. I was in the barracks that time. My girlfriend-- I was washing little potatoes. They needed help, so I remained.

INT: Then how did you get over the, the wall that surrounded--

RK: I didn't get over the wall, sir. I-- as I heard the German-- as I heard shooting, I must have gone out of my mind. Because I was screaming, my brother, my sister, my brother, my sister, where are they?

[SIGHS]

We had a window in the barracks. The people that were on the barracks with me pried the window open and jumped out. As one girl, she pushed-- one-- I was there, my cousin, and two sisters.

INT: But how did you get-- you had said that the camp was surrounded by a wall.

RK: Yes, but--

INT: Then how did you get past that wall?

RK: Because the window was facing the water, and it was on a hill. So they not-- they never expected that we will be able to, to run away from there. So this, this place was-- this wall was free.

And my friend saw that I'm out of my mind. She says, let's jump. I said, no, no, my brother, my sister. She didn't listen to me. She grabbed me by my blouse, and just pushed me through the window.

And I landed in the water. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I rolled, because our camp was high, and was a hill. And on the bottom of the hill was a river. I don't remember the name of the river.

It was November the 30th. Partially, the river was frozen, but not frozen enough that I fell, I should be dry. The ice broke. I was soaking wet. And from the tree, some kind of a splint-- nails got into my feet, because I had nothing on my feet. I was barefooted, not even the rags that I wore before. I thought, if I'm washing the potatoes, I'll let my rags dry, and, and I will put them on later.

And they dragged me into the forest. Through the fields, we ran away. My cousin says, if you don't walk, your feet will freeze, and then you'll lose your feet. I walked-- I don't know how-- on my-- on my knees. They were dragging me through the fields. And we got into the forest. Once we were in the forest, it wasn't far away.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I don't know how many blocks, but it wasn't very, very far away. We saw a light. This was still early in the morning. We saw a house. So I said to the other girls, there's a light. Somebody is there. Perhaps we could ring-- knock on the door, they'll let us in. In meantime, when I talked to my cousin and the other girl, two other girls came too. They escaped.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The people were kind enough, and opened the door. And they ask us, who are you? We told them that we are Jewish kids who escaped from the camp, and please let us in. At least let me put something-- and warm up my feet.

They let us in. They were Polish people. Let us in, and the woman was very, very sympathetic-- plain Polish farmers. And she gave us something to warm up. She had some milk, and kind of oatmeal she cooked. We were very grateful to her.

She kept us a little while. She said, I can't keep you too long, because I'm afraid myself. If my neighbors from the back saw you coming in, I'll be in trouble. We believed her.

As we were sitting there ready to go, three men walk in. Such giants! She said, don't be afraid, those are my brothers. And we told them too. They didn't say a word. We-- where are you going? We are going into the forest.

He advised us don't go too far. Because if you get lost, and those [NON-ENGLISH]-- I told you where the partisans went-- you'll never get out. So we told them, we have no choice. We have to go. Otherwise, maybe they're looking for us. Maybe-- we don't know.

We walked out. My group-- I, my cousin, and the two sisters-- stuck with me. But the other two ladies, they were from a big city, and older than us. They thought they are very smart. They're wiser, naturally. They said, no, we don't want to go with you. We'll go by ourself.

I said, if you'll get in deeper in the forest, you'll get lost, and you'll never get out. Did you hear what the man said? We don't believe him. I knew to believe him, because I come from a village. I know what it is. They walked away from us. We walked in a different direction, and they walked in a different direction.

We walked in deep, but facing, facing the-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the north. We knew where we came from. We came from the south, and, and the sun goes to the north. So if I go towards the south when I want to go out, I have to see where, where-- it's not the south. It's west, west-- where the sun goes down.

They went away, and we were standing-- sitting and sitting. All of a sudden, we hear screamings, calling us the names. They got lost. We-- by the voice, we found them, and we told them. Why did you separated?

Because you're a kid. You wanted to tell us where to go? We stood in the forest a whole day, not going out, but we, we knew which direction we have to go. When it got dark, we left the forest. Where do you go?

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Where do you go?

INT: Were you dry by now?

RK: I dried in the Polish house.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Where do you go? Oh, we'll stay in the forest. I said, then we'll stay in the forest. The Ukrainian people are cutting. They'll cut us to pieces. The only place to go is back to the barracks. Because we have no place to go. We'll freeze to death, or they'll cut us up to pieces.

We went back to the barracks. When I came, I was told that my sister escaped. My sister saved herself through the two pieces that she ripped off. And, and, and my brother-- no, he didn't. And he didn't survive.

What happened is Ukrainian men kept him, and he start screaming, oh, my sisters, they are not alive. Please let me out. He begged them on his knees. He says, no. My brother then took his head, and knocked in the wall, he should be half-conscious.

[PAUSES FOR 9 SECONDS]

[CRYING] And he was killed. How did I find out? Everything is from the girl-- a girl, a Jewish girl. I tell you how. She told me she saw my brother walking towards the grave.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

And he told her everything. If he would only have to let him out and run away. No. She survived because the gate was digged and they were going two people.

[PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

She was standing the third in the line. Two sisters were going. And then she jumped out from her line, and went into the middle. The two sisters were killed. And she was thrown alive in the grave.

[PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

From panic, her mouth turned here like this.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

But she could-- after the killing stopped, [PAUSES FOR 12 SECONDS] she climbed out from the grave.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

German soldiers saw it. Threw her-- threw her in the buggy and horse. They, they were some police they were driving there. And, and, and she got scared. And the German soldier says to her, [NON-ENGLISH]. Don't, don't be scared. We'll take you out. And they saved her life-- German soldiers.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

We came back to the-- to the barracks.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I didn't have a heart to go over to the grave where they killed my brother. They gave us-- not far away from there [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] from the barracks. We remained a few Jewish people. What do you do? Where do you go? We thought like this. Maybe they'll need us to work here. Maybe they'll leave us here.

INT: Can you help me to understand this? When the shooting started with the Germans, they didn't kill everybody in the camp there? When, when you were running away to escape, and the, the shooting started--

RK: Yeah, they did kill. I saw it, a mother and a daughter.

INT: No, did they kill all of the people who were living and working in that camp?

RK: Everybody. Some they killed while they were running, and some they took to the grave.

INT: Now when you came back to the barracks--

RK: Yeah.

INT: --then how come they were any people still alive in the camp there?

RK: Escaped. I escaped. [? Nusha ?] escaped. Her sister escaped. My cousin escaped. A little boy escaped. He worked in the stable.

INT: And these were the only people who came back?

RK: Only the people.

INT: So did you find a deserted camp there, or were the German, German officers still there?

RK: No, no.

INT: Or was nobody there?

RK: No. No, nobody was there.

INT: So you came back to an empty camp.

RK: That's right.

INT: Thank you for--

RK: And I forgot to tell you.

INT: Thank you for explaining that, OK.

RK: You're welcome. I forgot to tell you. When it got dark, as we were going back to the camp, I said the, the, the, the sun was very low. And on the the horizon, I noticed something moving. And that road was going straight from the camp-- where the camp was. We were here, and this was-- they were coming from there.

And I said to the girls, stop. My cousin says, what do you see now? I said, something is moving, and I'm not too happy. Because the farmers don't go so late home. She says, oh, Rachel, we go.

As they were coming closer-- I had luck that I was very far-sighted. I could see from very far away. I said, uh-oh, I don't like it. There three or four wagons. It means this may be the police, and that this may be the Germans going from the pogrom.

So I said, you know what? Let's hide in a ditch. In Europe is a road, and then deep ditches. This is for when it rains a lot that the water should not stay on the street, on the fields. There are ditches, the water goes there. We ditched, and, and, and we hid under these ditches.

We stuck out a little bit our, our head. The police, they were all drunk. And, and we looked. We almost fainted. This was the police leaving the pogrom. And this woman-- I don't remember her name. She was a beautiful woman, aristocratic-looking, Jewish-- says to me, Rachel, you are something.

I said, you know why I'm something? Because do you know what I lived through till before when-- until I came here to this camp? You, you came from the ghetto straight here. You don't know what this life is all about.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

When we came to the camp, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] there were only a few of us, just a few of us. We slept through the night. What shall we do? Meantime, they send in for workers. They had to shovel the snow. Because a lot of [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] trucks, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] horses and a buggy-- how do you cal that-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] on four wheels.

INT: A truck?

RK: Not trucks, that the, the horses drag.

INT: A sled?

RK: Big sleds.

INT: Right.

RK: No. It were going through the roads. And the Germans were taking whatever they could. Everything was going to Germany. So I said, you know what? If they come and shoot me here, I am not going to work. Finished. You see what we have what we worked so hard? I'm not going. I didn't go.

My cousin begged me in tears. Come, my sister, come. I said no. My work is finished. I'm not going to work. I said, I lost the most precious person-- one of my precious person in my life. My brother played for them, spoke German beautiful to them. And whenever they needed something, they called him.

Just, just something snapped in me. I'm not going, and I didn't go. For a week, we were-- we were in this camp.

[SIGHS]

INT: How did you eat during that week?

RK: What did we eat?

INT: How did you eat, yes?

RK: They still gave us rations.

INT: Who is they?

RK: The Germans.

INT: Excuse me, the Germans-- were they part of the camp staff, or just from the city around the camp, or what?

RK: No, the, the, the leader of the camp was a German. And I forgot his name. He wasn't so bad as his wife.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The pogrom was caused by her. She had called the Gestapo, and she says, the work is finished. We don't need the Jews anymore. A hateful--

INT: I'm sorry.

RK: Yes. Go ahead.

INT: But after the, the pogrom had been finished--

RK: Yeah.

INT: --I understand you to say that the, the barracks was deserted?

RK: Yes.

INT: That the camp was deserted. There's nobody.

RK: Yes, yes.

INT: Now then you said the Germans gave you rations for food. But I was understood that the Germans--

RK: From the camp-- from the camp-- the leader of the camp-- they had to feed us. They wanted us to work.

INT: Oh, so there were German administrators who were still in the camp?

RK: The, the main man was a German, sure.

INT: And they didn't shoot you when you came back after--

RK: No.

INT: --having escaped.

RK: No, no, no. No. The Germans that were killing us, they went to Chortkiv. Those are the Germans from the camp. This was two different things completely. The Germans who were there were soldiers. The Germans that came to kill us, this was the Gestapo, together with the Ukrainian police.

INT: I see.

RK: Two different categories.

INT: OK.

RK: The leader of that camp was not a bad man. It's the wife. How do we know? We found out through a Jewish man who was cutting his hair. You see, whoever had a trade was easier to survive, or make a living. He cut the leader's hair. He was an elderly man.

And he says that he feels very bad about the whole thing, but it's not his fault. It's his wife's fault. This what he told us, and I have to believe it. Because we knew her, and we knew him-- two different people.

After, just a few of us remained. What could we have done for them, anyway? They needed people to shovel the snow, and work. Who, kids? We couldn't. I refused to go to work anyway. So they decided they don't need us.

INT: What did your sister do at that moment?

RK: My sister was going to work.

INT: Oh.

RK: With my cousin-- my sister and my cousin didn't miss a day's work. My cousin-- for my cousin is meant to live, is the day of the pogrom I insisted she should not go, not knowing that what is going to happen.

I said, you faithfully work day by day. That little potatoes, at least, you help me wash. Because my hand used to swell up. And she says, Rachel, it's meant to, to-- for me to live. If my cousin would have gone to work, she wouldn't have survived.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

They decided to liquidate us, to, to send us away anyway. From there, they send us to Milowice.

[PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

We came to Milowice, we were not sure if they'll take us in or not. But they had an order to accept us. They needed people to work. There still were Jewish people there, a whole camp of people. But they lost a lot of people too. Because from their camp, they sent people to Lisowce when, when I told you the killing-- a lot of their people were killed. So they needed work.

So we came there, and they accepted us among Jewish people. We told them what happened. You know how it is, one helps the other. And we were staying there. We were staying, shoveling the same thing, going so far on roads, and such a cold winter days-- icy, not having nothing to wear, shoveling. Once you start working, you're not cold. But if you have something on your feet, at least your foot does not-- It, it doesn't touch the snow.

We worked. The fellows used-- worked in the stables took care of the horses. Other people worked on helping the cows. They always found work for us. Sometimes, it was raining terrible with snow, we had to work. And we worked.

An epidemic typhus hit camps too. So the Germans decided [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] wherever they'll find someone sick on the typhus, to shoot them in place. We had typhus, we were not worried about it.

One day-- one day, a doctor-- a Polish doctor-- came in with a nurse. We all went to work. Just one woman remained there. She was wounded in a pogrom before. She said, I'll stay, pretending I'm cooking, taking care. Because somebody had to cook some soup. And you all go to work. I don't want you here. We went away.

We saw the doctor come in with a nurse. And as we were shoveling snow around the barracks, we heard cries, laughter. We didn't know, or she's hysterical, or she's happy, or she's crying. We didn't know what happened. We waited anxiously the doctor with the nurse should go away. They went away, we came into the-- to, to the barracks.

She said, Rachel, you wouldn't believe it. I said, what happened? This is the Polish doctor. Came to look at somebody sick. He made my oldest daughter papers-- Christian papers. Send her to Lwów. And they are in touch. This is my doctor.

I said, my God. I said, we were scared to death. We didn't know what happened. I have such good news. Oh God, she says, spare our lives. I, I'll still see my, my daughter.

[PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

It, it-- we were very happy to hear about it.

