That it's getting more dangerous to get out of the camp. Because if you leave a camp, the Ukrainian [? Bandera ?] officers started a war against us. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Nevertheless, in a short time, we were sent. We were sent out. We-- we were send out. [SIGHS]

So we were send out to [NON-ENGLISH], Szypowce-- Szypowce, a camp. It was very far away from there. They send us there to work.

This-- again, this was a camp that once, Jewish people were working. No more. Everybody was killed.

They needed help. We were there to help them. We worked. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

What did we do? What do you do winter? You do the same thing. You shovel snow, work on the-- work-- work in the barn, carry heavy potatoes, clean the snow. Whatever we-- we had to do, we did. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

We didn't stay too long. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We heard rumors that the Ukrainians are killing our people in every camp, just liquidating them. Just killing them.

The Germans didn't bother with us, because they had to do it [? with ?] [? themself. ?] It looks like-- we didn't know about it-- that America proclaimed war. The Russians were advancing. The Germans had to do it themself. We Jewish people were not so important to them anymore.

But we had another enemy to fight. And this was a terrible enemy. In one camp, they killed everybody. A girl, [? Esther ?] [NON-ENGLISH], she's in Israel now, and another little girl, those are the two survivors, as far as I know.

We were-- we were being scared. We were very scared. But at the end, there's nothing we can do.

So we had to take [SIGHS] the way it is and praying to God that the liberation will come soon. We did not sleep in the barracks. One night, we sneaked in-- into the stable.

The stable was very warm. Nobody watched us. And we sneaked in-- into the stable.

As we went to sleep, the doors [? pried ?] open, and the Ukrainian [? Bandera ?] officers came in, all-- all [? with the ?] rifles. Where are the Jews? Where are the Jews?

There's no other Jews. We're all kids here, working. And there are not too many of us.

As he was talking, as he went to the men first-- usually, they used to go to the men first-- I sneaked out, barefooted. Just what I had on me. What-- what-- what did I have on me? A skirt, not even a jacket, nothing. I-- some kind of piece of junk on me.

Barefooted, I sneaked out. Ran away. I couldn't ran anymore. I hid in a hole.

This-- I didn't know that Jewish people were buried there. And I sat. I sat 100% about an hour. And I was getting very cold. And my feet were sticking to the ice.

I said, my God, if I go to sleep, oh, my foot will stick to the ice. I'll never make it back to the camp.

I took my skirt and covered my feet. And I sat shivering. It's cold.

I said to myself, I don't care if they kill me or not. I'm going back to the barracks-- to the stable. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I couldn't walk anymore. I had to crawl.

My sister, my cousin were hysterical. They thought they lost me, because they didn't know what happened. They didn't know if they took me away. They didn't know what happened.

I just sneaked out, because it happened, that time that they came in, it went right onto the left. And I was sleeping to the right in the side. So it was very easy for me to sneak out.

I came back to the barracks. My-- my sister wanted to help me. I know you can't put warm stuff on the foot, on the feet, because your feet are frozen. They brought in snow and rubbed my snow-- snow on my feet. My feet came alive.

I thought I'll never walk again. I thought I'll never walk again. And we-- we said to each other, you know what? This is not-- this-- this will be our end here, if we don't do something. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Meantime, as they send us away from that camp Milowce, the work stopped. They needed us. They send words we should go back.

They send-- they send a buggy and a horse for us. They took us back to Milowce. We thought we are back in heaven. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Immediately, they gave us better food. And they put us to work. So I said to the people there, you see? They need us.

They cannot manage without us. Because Jewish people gave them free labor. And if they took their people, the Ukrainians or the Poles, they had to pay them. And we worked for nothing.

But the situation didn't change. The Germans didn't bother us, as I told you before. We had another enemy, the Ukrainians. Excuse me. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

One man, a-- one man, a Jewish man, befriended a man in-- in-- in the village. He used to sneak out. And he went to see him.

A few times, he saw him there. He played cards with him. He made friends. The last time he sneaked out, he never came back.

We had a young man. [? Hoschler ?] was his name. He was a-- a tailor. Tailors were needed among the non-Jewish. He went into the village to do some work. Sneaked out. For a piece of bread, he sneaked out to do some work.

He came back hysterical. He says somebody was following him. And he's not going anymore.

The Jewish people took-- sent somebody to the Ukrainian priest to talk to him-- we saw that the Germans left us, already, sort of alone-- and begged him, please, if you have a sermon, a speech, whatever you call it in church, please, tell your people we are not the enemies. We are not Russian lovers.

We just want to live. That's all. Please, we are begging you. Tell them to leave us alone.

The-- the priest answer was I'm not mixing in in politics. But he said, this is not politics. We are not politicians, either. We just want to live.

The answer was, you are suffering because you-- you killed our God, Jesus Christ. He saw it's trouble. He saw it's trouble.

So [SIGHS] we start-- we all got together. And we-- and we say to each other, it's no good. If they killed in so many camps, people, they'll go after us, too. They will not let us live.

But what do you do? What do you do? Some people had a little money yet. Used to sneak out from the camp, go to the people, pay a few zlotys. I don't know what kind of money they had.

So the non-Jewish people, they kind of let him slip through, [? over. ?] But we didn't have it. Maybe one or two went.

We didn't have it. And the only thing is just to believe strong in God. And God can only help us.

It was a Sunday. We heard the Russians are progressing. The war is going very well for us, not for the Germans. After church, we noticed so many Ukrainians are coming to-- to the camp and talk to us, talk to us.

So-- so this [? Hoschler ?] says, you see? Maybe he did have a speech. And they want to make friends with us. Who would expect anything else?

And a woman walked over to me. She wanted to tell me something. But I probably didn't understand her. Warn me or something, I don't remember.

And we came to camp. And-- and something was in the air that everybody knew, if not tonight, it's tomorrow. Not tomorrow, it going to be a day later. They'll do something to us.

We came into camp. And it got dark. All of a sudden, everybody disappeared.

I remained. A father and a son remained. The kid was sick. I, my cousin, my sister, [NON-ENGLISH], a boy [INAUDIBLE], and a little boy, a little boy. Which he smuggled himself in, and whenever we went to work, he didn't show his face. Because he was not allowed to be in camp.

And then my sister started to cry hysterically. Everybody run away. We have no money to get away.

And they are going to kill us all. Oh, Rachel, they're going to kill us all. We're not going to live.

I said calm down. There's nothing we can do. We have no place where to go.

As I was talking to her, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I hear footsteps outside. Uh oh. But I didn't want to scare my sister.

A few Ukrainian men came into the barracks and looked around. Oh, where are the rich Jewish people? They run away.

Not all of them ran to the village. There was a cellar in camp. They used to storage the potatoes for the cows.

They opened that cellar. And a lot of people went in there and locked themself. And they thought that never in the world will anybody go there. They wouldn't know.

Two brothers, they were from [? Zalishchyky. ?] Sorry, I don't remember their names, very nice fellows. Made themself a hiding place [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] in-- in-- over the barracks, in the corner of the barracks, whatever it is. They separated the boards and went in there and hid.

But we didn't know about it. They didn't tell anybody. Because if they'll catch me, they were afraid somebody else reveal the place. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

They came in. And they said-- those Ukrainians came in. And they say, where are the Jews? And we said, I don't know.

Among them was a man that my brother used to tutor him, his son. And he improved tremendously in school. When he found out my brother was killed, he cried.

He didn't [INAUDIBLE]. He just turned around about his eyes. Such a brilliant boy, how could they do this?

And he says, why is your sister crying? I said to him-- what could I tell him? I told them that her teeth hurt.

So he says, why don't you go on [INAUDIBLE]? Like this, with eyes, why don't you go village? You know our healings, the village-- village healings.

If you take them wax from the candle, when somebody dies, by the Catholics, they put them on a table, and they light two candles. Take the wax that drips, put-- put to her tooth. The pain will go away.

He gave me a sign I shall disappear. As soon as they went away, as soon as they walked out, they didn't do nothing to us. They walked out.

And I said, this is [? nicht ?] [? gut. ?] It's no good. I have a sign to go.

They looked at me. I said, we are leaving. And the father and the son couldn't go because the son was sick. Where do we go? I said, [NON-ENGLISH]-- I took along this fellow. He was about 40 years old.

I says, [NON-ENGLISH], you are going with us, and the kid, and my sister, I. My cousin went with another woman. I said, let's go.

As we walked out, lights-- you know those lights? How do you call them?

INT: Search lights?

RK: Search lights were after us. But we threw ourselves on the ground. If you stay up, then they see you. But if you put yourself on the floor, nobody can see you.

And I went towards the village. I noticed once that there is a stable there, always open, always open. Whenever we pass by from work or to work, open.

So I said, let's go to the stable. I said, I know where to go. Here's a stable.

The stable's always open. We'll sneak in. She wouldn't even know.

That night, the stable was closed. We came, the stable's closed. So I said to my-- to [NON-ENGLISH] and my sister, I'm going to beg the woman she should open the stable.

Maybe-- maybe you-- if I'll tell you something, maybe you'll laugh or something. But that's the honest truth.

As-- as I opened-- as I picked up my hand to knock on the door, something pulled me that I fell. I fell. And I said to them, why are you pulling me?

They said, Rachel, we didn't touch you. As I fell, I noticed on the side, to the left, a grave with potatoes. You know what I mean.

They dig a hole, the Ukraine people, and put the potatoes and cover with straw to make, like, a roof. They make an opening. You go inside. And you can pick potatoes. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I noticed that grave of potatoes. I said, you're going there. I couldn't talk. We climbed in-- into the grave of potatoes. We covered ourselves with straw. And we fell asleep.

As I-- as I was thinking, my mom was with me. This was my mom pulling me, saying, don't knock. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] [SOBBING] I always knew that she was with me. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

We live through the night and the morning. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It wasn't-- it wasn't yet morning, but my sister was waking me. And she says, Rachel, get up.

I said what happened? I hear something. I put my ear towards the wall from that place, the grave.

You know how the Indians, when you put the-- the ear to the-- to the ground, you hear? This how you can find out if somebody is looking for you. My God, I could hear steps of horses, steps of people. They were looking for us. Because they saw me going there.

But never like they imagined that I-- I-- would hide in a cold, ice cold grave of potatoes. We got up in the morning, early in the morning, not to show the leader of the camp that we were hiding. And there, a little snow fell. In the snow was [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] steps of horses, steps of people. And I said to myself, my God what-- what was it [? true ?] they were looking for us?

I came to the camp. Nobody came back. I didn't find the-- the son. I didn't find the father. Everything was broken, camp, everything. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I said to myself, oh, my God. Something must have happened. Going from that woman, I went to a house.

And the woman saw me. She crossed herself. She said, where were you?

I said, what happened? She said, I can't talk to you and slammed the door. She got scared.

I came to the camp. I didn't find nobody. My cousin came with the woman back.

And those two brothers came down. They were hiding, I told you, under the roof. And they jumped at me, embraced me, and said to me, oh, kids, thank God that you alive.

I said, what happened? Where's everybody? Don't go to the-- to the cellar, where the-- where all the potatoes from the-- from the camp are hidden there. I said, what happened?

There were screams during the night. They slaughtered everybody, OK? Those are our friends.

INT: Everybody in Milowce?

