All-- a few girls-- all-- we were a few girls. They climbed in the attic and took the ladder upstairs. I could not climb. I just couldn't. My feet were swollen. So I grabbed my sister by her hand and I said, let's get out.

As I walked out, I saw the Cossack going over to that shack. But this was my luck, that the war was coming to an end. Everything was falling apart. The villagers came to rob the camp. They were taking cows, horses. They were taking little piglets, little piggies, whatever they could get a hold of to take it back from the Germans, what the Germans took from them.

INT: Did you know at this point that the war was now coming to an end?

RK: Yes, yes. We didn't know when, but we knew it's not too long. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We met some Hungarian soldiers there. And they told us if we'll pull out, in a few days, you'll have the Russians here. So as I couldn't get into the attic because my feet were swollen, I grabbed my sister's hand and I said, let's get out of here.

As I walked out, I saw a few Ukrainian women holding little piggies. I and my sister walked over and I said to my sister, don't look back. The Cossacks are going right in our direction. I don't know how safe they are there, but don't look back. And I turned towards the Ukrainian women, and I said to them-- I spoke fluently Ukrainian.

They probably knew I was Jewish, but they didn't pay any attention. The women were not as, as bitter as the men. And I, and I start to pet the piglets. And my sister stood near me. And those poor Ukrainians were not better dressed than I was. While true, I was in rags, I had a skirt made from a sack, they, they wore just almost the same.

And I started to talk. They understood. They didn't say a word. The Cossack ran into that shack. He didn't see nobody because they were hiding in the attic. And he ran out, looked at our side, looked towards us. He saw that those are not--

INT: Excuse me.

RK: --gesundheit-- those are not Jewish people. They're holding piglets. And he ran away. I sunk to the floor. I sat down on the floor. I just couldn't-- my, my feet did not hold me up. We survived again, a very, very close, close call.

The girls came down from the attic and they said, we are so sorry, but truly there was no time to drag you up. And I said I couldn't walk up. I just couldn't. And my cousin joined me. And I said, you know what, let's go and talk to people, what's going on.

As I asked her let's go and talk to the people, one Hungarian soldier was going towards us. We didn't know who he was. He was a Jewish soldier, taken into the army, not to fight anybody, but just to dig ditches for the Germans. And he says, you're Jewish? I said, sure. But we were dressed like those farmers, girls. And we exchanged a few words.

And he says to me like this. Do not remain here, if we are pulling out, pull out like us. Because we are going from camp to camp. The main road was going this way. If you remain here, the Ukrainians will slaughter you. I thanked him.

What do we do? We decided to go. Where did we go? I reminded myself that in our camp we had a family, Mrs. [? Margolis. ?] She was a dear, dear woman, very nice. She was a sweetheart. She lost her husband. Her husband was a tailor. He worked for, for the Ukrainian police-- the Ukrainian police. This what all these pogroms he was safe, but then he died on typhus-- and her son and two daughters.

I befriended one of the daughters and I was friends with her son too. And she always used to say, kids, if we survive, you are coming to live with us. They loved-- they loved us. We were quiet kids, not fighting with nobody. And my brother was respected by everybody. He was a quiet, nice, young man. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I said to my cousin, let's go to her. Let's go in the camp Tluste. We heard that there is a very, very good German, a good German. After the war, there were rumors-- I'm not so sure, they say that he was a Jewish German, a Jewish man. He was Jewish, but a German-- a Jewish German. We didn't know anything.

But the only thing that we heard, that never, never since I'm in the camps, we went through so much, nobody was killed there. Once the police came, they wanted to make a pogrom. He says, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] stop, I need the Jewish people to work. He didn't let nobody to be slaughtered, killed. So I said to my cousin. Let's go there. We know the, the daughter, we know the people. At least we know somebody.

And we decided to go. This was late in the afternoon, late. The German army and the Hungarian soldiers were going with the main road. I, my sister, my cousin, and another man with small kids-- not small kids, young boys-- was going in the front. He didn't want to walk with us. He says it'll be too much. So I said, you go your own way, I'll go my own way. He survived too.

And as we were walking, the, the soldiers looked at us. And we waved our hands. Means that, we greet them. We're your friends. Here, here in the place were watching how the army was pulling back. And we were walking. Nobody bothered us. A lot of people remained and a lot of people followed us later.

We walked as much as we could with them. They turned-- the main road turned to the left and we went to the right. We went through fields. Oh, God, dearest, in March, to go through fields, everything is watery. It's not frozen. I couldn't-- I couldn't pull out my legs with my feet. My cousin and my sister were honestly physically dragging me. Because my feet froze because I stood on, on the snow.

As we were approaching I said the safest place we-- way we should go is through the Jewish cemetery. Nobody will expect anybody going there. When we came to the cemetery, I stopped. And I couldn't walk. Those mass graves overtook me. I, I was torn. I was never during the war on a Jewish cemetery.

And my cousin said, oh, Rachel, come. She pulled me and we went. We went to the edge. We look down. We see the camp. My cousin says, oh, thank God, how do we get in? We walked down the hill. The camp was-- with lights all around the hill.

And I said, you know what, we'll go one by one. One goes in, then the second. We should not go three together. This way they may think somebody walked out and walking back. And this what we did. As soon as we walked in, in camp, we opened a door from the barracks.

I have never seen a thing like this. There were hundreds of people. They found out that this German has a good heart and nobody was ever killed in his camp. Everybody who remained alive aimed toward this camp.

INT: Now, how-- even though you went in one by one, how did you get in there? Were there--

RK: The door was open.

INT: --no gates?

RK: Yes, there was, but the door was open.

INT: No guards there at the door to stop people?

RK: No, because the German army was on the grounds of the camp. The Germans themself-- the city was destroyed, completely. The ghetto was in-- the camp was in the-- close to ghetto. So where shall they stop? They had no place where to stop, the Germans. So they saw a free place and they stopped there, which this was our salvation.

And the director of the camp was a German. He, he lived in such a beautiful house. To me, it was like the White House. It was beautiful white painted. And they stopped there. They opened a kitchen, a soldier's kitchen. I don't know-- such big pots. And they were cooking, killing cows there.

So my cousin says, here we are safe. At least the Germans-- the Ukrainians will not come. And we went in the barracks-- so many people. Where are you from? From this camp. Where are you from? From that camp, and this camp, and that camp.

I said how did you come here? We, we had no place where to go, so we came here. We heard this German is a-- is a-- has a heart.

INT: At that point, was there still a Judenrate there--

RK: No, no.

INT: --that was assigning housing and things like that?

RK: No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no. The ghetto was destroyed. Only camps existed, no Judenrate, no Jewish police. The Jewish police always thought by performing a good job, they'll be safe. By catching all Jewish people that the Germans needed, they'll be acknowledged as good. To the end, they shot them just like they shoot everybody.

INT: Now--

RK: I'm not finished.

INT: Go ahead, I'm sorry.

RK: So we were there. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Again, we have to eat. What do you do? I walked out. I-- a man recognized me. They used to call us kids. Oh, my God, I'm so happy you survived. He was in one of the camps with me. I'm so glad you survived, how are you?

I said, we are-- I lost my brother, I told him, but we are in bad shape. We have nothing to eat. Not only nothing to eat, we don't even have a pot. If, if you find a potato someplace, or-- I was rich, I had six zlotys. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I have nothing to cook in.

He says, you have a pot. He was, himself, how do you call it? [NON-ENGLISH], means a man that pots. He says you'll have a pot. Give me a half an hour. He went and took off a piece of aluminum from the corner of one of the camps, made us a pot. I'm in business, I have a pot. But what do you cook?

I went into the stable. I sneaked into the stable where they fed the cows. I said, if there are cows and nobody's watching, they must eat something. What do they eat? Cows ate very well potatoes. I-- I looked where they were eating, little piece of potatoes. Oh, my God, [INAUDIBLE] took. Put it in my pockets. And I'm rich.

Then I noticed a piece, a piece of salt. They used to give the cows black salt to, how do you call it-- to chew. It was very healthy for them. I even stole a little piece of salt. And my cousin says, if they'll find-- if they'll see this missing, they'll come after us. I said, hundreds of people in that camp, hundreds, so they'll know that I took it?

I came-- so I had potatoes. And I had a piece of salt. But water-- water was not clean. There was a little stream going through. I don't know, maybe somebody even did something there, but we couldn't care less. I went and got some water. I looked for a deeper spot to fetch a little water. I had-- I found a bottle. I broke the bottle so I could-- with the bottom, you know, cut off, I could dip it in and take out some water.

I took a half a pot of water. I washed my potatoes, put it in three rocks, and I cooked. In meantime, the Germans, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the director of the camp, gave a order to give the people, each one, a cup of cornmeal. So we all stood in the line and got cornmeal. It was a luxury.

And I cooked. There, I didn't even know some kids survived-- survived didn't have anything to eat too. So everybody ate a little bit. And I was-- at least we had something to eat.

I sent-- I met somebody and I asked them, do you know the [? Margolis ?] family? Yes. I said, tell them that I'm here with my sister and my cousin. So he says, I have sad news for you. The mother was killed. She was sick on typhus and they shot her. But the little girl lives, the sister, and the fellow lives. Immediately, they came to see me.

What can we help you? I said, what can you help me. I have a few zlotys. I would like to buy a potato, something. They said, you can't. Just what we can get is eggs, three eggs. I said, OK, at least I'll have three eggs. And we stood there. I think we were almost a week, maybe less, I don't know.

The Germans were cooking, killing cows and cooking soup. It smelled so delicious. And one day they brought in a full pot, those, those pots are big, what the soldiers cook in it, bones. And we threw ourselves on that, those bones. Everybody was grabbing a bone.

And this German-- German fella looks, and he says, my [NON-ENGLISH] God, my dearest God, who are you, he says. And he looks around in, in the barrack. There were so many people, so many people, one next to the other. The facilities, it was unbearable.

But we knew it's not going to last forever. So whoever could find a little corner lie down to save himself. And he walked away. So I said to somebody, oh, boy, now I'm scared. Because he came in and saw all this, what's going on, he may think somebody's sick on typhus and they're going to shoot us. They didn't even pay any attention to us.

Only the second time a German came in, is-- not didn't come in, he stood, and because the people had to go outside. And he was screaming [NON-ENGLISH], hide, because the Gestapo's passing by. They didn't want to, to endanger our lives, which we were very shocked and surprised.

Then in the morning, we were in touch with the-- there were Hungarian soldiers there too. And we were, we were talking to them too. And he says to, to us, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] there are rumors that the Russians are very close. When we pack in two, three hours, you'll be liberated. We just couldn't believe it. We just couldn't believe it.

All of a sudden, appell, start whistle blowing and the Germans are packing. But I forgot to tell you that the same morning, the director of the camp that was so nice, packed his family and told the police in-- in-- the Ukrainian police, to not touch the Jewish people. I'm taking my wife to the train in [NON-ENGLISH], to send her to Germany, to her mom-- mother, and I'm coming back. And I'll keep you responsible.

He never came back. He escaped. But nobody knew. I think that whoever knew didn't want to tell us, not to make a big panic in camp.

Here this what he told us, once we move they're coming, the Russians. It's in the afternoon. No Russians, the Germans are still here. They're running away. And we said, my God, if we'll have to sleep through a night without the-- without the Russians coming in, and the Germans, and, and the Hungarians, the Ukrainians will come and slaughter us.

Some people were ready to go to the forest, but who wants to go to the forest, so bitter cold. And we'll be more open to be killed than here in the barracks. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] We, we, we decided not to go. My sister was sick. She had temperature. She caught a cold from all this. And I-- and I told the other people, I-- we are not going. We are staying here. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

It was getting-- the-- the, the shooting from the cannons were getting louder, and louder, and louder. All of a sudden we walk out. A drunken German is standing. And he says [GERMAN], we shouldn't be afraid of him. I'll protect you, he says. Nothing will happen to you. We didn't even know who he was.

But we said to ourselves, a German should protect us, oh, my God, something is-- something good has happened. And we all closed the barracks. And we stood in the barracks. The cannons-- the shooting of the cannon-- the cannons was coming closer. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And all of a sudden, we heard a noise like tanks. We didn't know if this is Russian tanks or German tanks. Nevertheless, the people broke the doors and ran out. Russian tanks, I can't tell you the emotion what it was.

INT: All right, before-- before we go into, into that, you've told us a great deal about your experiences, both in the ghettos and the camps. Is there any-- are there any experiences that you have wanted to share with us that we have overlooked before we go into the liberation period?

RK: What happened, my cousins-- we didn't know about that, but I'll-- I can add this. My cousin's parents went back from the ghetto, Buczacz-- from the ghetto Tluste back home to Ostra. They were hiding out. It was two-- a father, mother, the two little sisters, a aunt, and a, and a little boy. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] His name was [? Leib. ?]

I told you when they took us to the German commandant, to the-- in Buczacz, when they send us from Koropiec to Buczacz, to the-- to the Kommandantur, I told him, go tell Mr. Friedental that we are being taken there. And he was there.

This fellow who caught us, Ivan Herasimovich, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] gave out that they were hiding in the village. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Ukrainian police, together with the Germans, came and shot them, all of them. Only this little boy escaped. He escaped and they couldn't shoot him because people were standing around when he escaped.

The Ukrainian people caught this boy-- this was why my husband sending me the note. Bind him with a wire. Took him to the floor in a terrible, terrible heat. And he was begging for, for water, please, just let me go. He died.

INT: How old was he?

RK: He must have been 10, mostly 10 years.

INT: OK, let's come back to the day of liberation.

RK: Yeah.

INT: You started to tell us about the emotion that you felt.

RK: It was happiness and sadness. Sadness, because nobody more lived through. But we didn't know what's waiting us. We are liberated, but we didn't know what's going to happen to us. All of a sudden, the German planes came on. I don't remember, one or two, not too many. And, and the Russian soldiers from the tanks started to scream to us, [RUSSIAN]. It means run, there's an attack.

We thought they're going to attack the tanks. So we ran back to the barracks. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] As I run to the barracks, those Germans were shooting at us.

INT: In the airplanes?

RK: From the airplanes, throwing bombs and shooting at us. I reached the door from the barracks. My sister was in front of me. I was next, because I let her go first. She was younger than I. And a woman was pushing me. We were pushing each other. My sister was wounded.

It's not only wounded. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Her whole face was torn off. God dearest, I didn't even know. Because [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] as I was-- I didn't know. She was first. And I was behind her. But the woman was stronger than I, and she pushed me, she covered me. She was killed. I survived.

But in all the confusion, I didn't see my sister. I didn't know what happened to her. I walked out of the barracks. I pulled out from the barracks-- where's my sister? As I was walking, there were shooting without a stop, the Germans. A piece of wood from the barracks broke off and hit-- a nail hit me here. I still have marks here.

From the panic, I pulled this out and blood-- you know, when you hit your head, a lot of blood comes out anyway. I couldn't see. I was bleeding terribly. But I pressed my hand here. And I remember, my brother used to say, if you get hurt and it's bleeding, take some earth-- how do you say-- mud. The mud stops immediately the bleeding.

This what I did. This why I didn't bleed to death. A lot of my people bled to death from wounds from bullets. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] [SIGHS] They were running for individual-- for individual people, the Germans. They just flew to liquidate the camps, not to leave a sign what was happening, families, people.

In the barrack that I was previously, nobody survived. One barrack was next to the other. In the first barrack that I was, nobody survived. In the second, a lot, a lot of were wounded. At that time, about 400 people were killed.

INT: Did the Russians and the tanks attempt to fire back at these planes?

RK: Oh, yeah, they did fire, sure. But--

INT: But they didn't-- apparently did not scare them away.

RK: They had-- they, the, the Germans were very advanced in building the airplanes. They had very good planes. This what the Russians always said, that they were scared of the, of the German planes. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I escaped from the barrack and I ran towards the fields.

INT: OK, this is the end of this tape. And we'll take a break for the next tape.

RK: OK.INT: This is tape number 12 in the interview with Mrs. Rachel Kriegel. You were starting to tell us about what happened after you got up from the airplane attack on the barracks.

RK: I was searching for my sister. I didn't see her in the barracks. Because there were so many people, there you couldn't see anything. I ran out from the barracks. I told myself, I better get out of here.

The, the plane-- the German planes were going after every individual person. They didn't just-- they were just there to, to liquidate everything, any sign that ever was there. They bombed the barracks. There was a church next to the barrack liquidated completely.

And I told them-- I said, the best thing is to be on the free fields, empty fields. And so I was running with my cousin. Other people came along, and the priest too-- a priest from Tluste that I heard he was a very, very nice man, very nice person. Matter of fact, the last week before the liberation, one, one of our people was hiding in his place.

We were running towards the fields. As we were running-- again, as I told you before, I'm far-sighted. I can see things from very far away. I told again, I-- something is moving on the horizon. And my cousin again says to me, you always see. I said, [NON-ENGLISH], something is moving. It may be German tanks, or maybe Russians. We don't know. As-- it took a second, a split-- a split second.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The Russian tanks were going towards us, but so much-- so much-- I don't know. Not experience, but just God has given some brains in my head-- not to lie on the floor. I said, let's get up. If this is the Russians, they'll think that we-- the Germans are going counter shoot-- counter attack. Let's get up.

And I got up. I took off my rag that I bandaged my head, full with blood, and I start waving like this. And I said to all of them, get up, get up. Those are Russian tanks. There were elderly people. Maybe they didn't see so far. Even the priest got up. It took a split second.

The tanks were close to us. The soldiers jumped down, and he said, who are you? I was ready to give an order to shoot. He thought that those are Germans going to counter attack us. And we told them who we are, and I begged him he should take me on the tank. I couldn't walk.

He says, no, we have a order to take nobody. This is Stalin's order. Because we're in a territory that we don't know who is our friend, and who is our enemy. The tanks went away, and we went back to the camp. We went back to the camp. We didn't sleep in the barracks, barracks. There were no more barracks. I searched for, for my sister. I found her.

I have no enemy in this world [CRYING] to wish on somebody what I found. In my heart, I said to myself, I wish she would have been killed. Her face-- her face was torn off. I took her in my arms.

INT: Was she still alive at that point?

RK: Yeah, sure, she was alive. And I said, oh God dearest, if only a foot or an arm would have been hurt, and she could live. I couldn't drag her. I couldn't take her out. So I asked my friend that he-- they-- they all were before in camp with me. I called him, and he helped me take her out.

He carried her in, in his arms, and he brought her to the place where the-- where the German director of the camp lived. It was nice and warm, and I lie her down. She wanted a mirror to see. I said, I don't have a mirror. I didn't want to give her.

And we slept through the night there. The poor girl was-- you know what it is to be wounded. We didn't sleep. We were sitting.

In the morning, [SIGHS] as God is my witness, I'm telling the truth. It was a beautiful day. Where the German lived, such beautiful flowers were going-- coming out from the ground because it was March. [CRYING] I walked over to the Russian soldier, and I told him, would do me a favor, he will shoot me.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So he looks at me. He thought I was out of my mind. I said, I truly don't want to live. He says, we liberated you. I said, yes. I took his hand, and I took him over to my sister. And I showed him my sister.

He said that they're going to take the wounded people to Chortkiv, to hospital. I'll see to it that she's taken away there. Some were wounded that, that you could help. But her?

[SIGHS]

What can I tell you? Maybe if the Americans would have liberated us, maybe with the knowledge that the American doctors have, would have been maybe different. I don't know. And, and who do you think ran that hospital? Not our friends. The same people that worked for the Germans. I didn't expect from them too much.

I looked for her. I couldn't find her. I went the second day to the hospital, I found her.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I can tell you, I was crushed to pieces. She looked at me, and what could I say to her? I said, you'll get better. She didn't have a mirror, she didn't see herself so much. She couldn't see herself. And we went back to the city, Chortkiv. We took over a place where the Germans lived before-- all the Jewish people. We escaped from-- we went from Tluste to Chortkiv.

INT: How far was Chortkiv?

RK: About 12 kilometers.

INT: And how did you get there?

RK: Walked. Because there were rumors that the German army, who was surrounded near [? Kharkiv ?] [? Kiev-- ?] means Ukrainian, but on the Russian side-- are trying to push themselves through to their main army. These were the rumors, and that's what the Russians told us. So I said to my friends, my sister is in Chortkiv, in the hospital. I'm leaving, I'm going.

Everybody-- some, some people ran away to Czernowitz in Romania. I, I had no reason, and I couldn't. From, from Tluste to Czernowitz is very far. Chortkiv was closer.

Middle of the night, you know, such a storm. That day, in the evening, was a storm. A whole night, we walked. The Russians didn't want to take us on the trucks. We had a order not to take anybody. We don't know who you are. You may say you're [INAUDIBLE] If maybe I would have met a Jewish soldier, spoke to him Jewish, he would have trusted me.

Yes, and during the liberation-- I forgot to tell you-- [SIGHS] when we were liberated, and running to the-- when we were liberated, a Russian Jewish soldier all of a sudden start crying, and embracing everybody. He says to us, I went through whole Ukraine, I didn't find a Jewish soul. You are the first Jewish people that I found. He was killed among the other ones too.

Now I'll go back to the other thing. We walked a whole night in bitter cold. Bitter cold, but in the snow. The worst thing was the snow. So we decided to, to walk behind the tanks. And they talk, so they make a pad. Otherwise-- I was never in Chortkiv. How will I find it?

We arrived in Chortkiv, not only I, but then I met a lot of people there. And I saw my sister. I told her I'm going to come the next day. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Rumors-- everybody's running. The Germans are coming.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

What was I supposed to do? I didn't want to go. Everybody said, are you crazy? You cannot help your sister. Come. My sister, when I saw her the last time, she was not-- I knew she's not going to live.

I saw her. The nurse says another day, and that's it. [SIGHS] I said goodbye to her, and I said, I'll come to see you, and she didn't even answer me. Oh, like this. She couldn't talk. And that's it. And I escaped.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I had very mixed feelings about it. I didn't know what to do. I escaped. I went on to [? Borstow. ?] From [? Borstow, ?] we went to Skala. It was [? Borstow-- ?] Podwoloczyska. From Podwoloczyska, we went to Skala. Skala was near the Russian border.

INT: You never saw your sister again?

RK: I came back. I went to the hospital. The nurse told me, do not despair. She was better off-- she died-- than be this way. I went through hell. I can't even describe you what I went through. I had a guilty feeling too. Because why did I leave her?

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Why? Everybody says, so what would you have done? The, the nurse told me she's not going to last.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

We stood there till we heard that the, the German army cut through, but omitted certain cities. And I went back to Chortkiv. Chortkiv, I settled. I lived there with girls-- with the 40 girls that I showed you-- I lived with the girl-- with girls.

When my place was liberated-- my home-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I went back.

INT: How did you get back?

RK: Trucks, on the Russian trucks. I felt-- I felt there was nothing for me to do here. I have nothing. I know that my mother gave away things. And in my heart, I had one thing, so I'm going to get my father's ring, and my-- and my mother's chain back. I made a promise to myself and to them.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

[SIGHS]

My place was liberated, but it took quite a few months. We went home to Koropiec. There, I met my father's cousin, and his son, and the niece and the nephew. And this Polish girl saved their life. And this how I know that she saved them. Because--

INT: Just very briefly, how did she save them?

RK: She, she digged a basement in the stable, kept them during the day in the-- in the basement. And at night, they used to go out in the stable and sit, have a little fresh air.

INT: Do you remember what her name was?

RK: Yes, [? Juse ?] Jowarska-- Jusefa Jowarska.

INT: And what did you do next?

RK: I stood in Koropiec, and I aimed to go back home. When I was approaching the place where I was born-- outskirts-- through the-- I was-- the main road, and then were fields-- the people that worked in the fields, and they kneeled and crossed themselves. They thought it's a ghost coming. Some were friendly, some were not.

I came and went to my neighbor's-- to our neighbor, that my mother saved her life. How did she save? Excuse me. She had something in her breast. I told you this before. So I told myself, to whom do I go? I'll say hello to her. And we went to say hello to a few people that we knew.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Disregarding that-- that her son was the one that caught us and sent us to the police. I didn't think of it, and I didn't want-- I pretended it's nothing. I blocked it out. I didn't block it out of my mind, but I just didn't want-- I, I just did want to show that I-- that I lived this through to her.

INT: How did she greet you?

RK: Very warmly. Yes, she was my, my friend-- my mother's very good friend. And her father and her-- they were friends from the First World War. Her father saved my mother from [? Kazakhs. ?] And she was not a plain Ukrainian person, but she was not-- the poor woman [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] didn't have luck. She married a, a man that was not for her, and this what it is. And she had a-- they were six, seven-- she had seven kids.

INT: Did you ever recover your father's ring--

RK: Yes.

INT: --and your mother's locket? Did you have any trouble getting it back?

RK: They didn't want to give me back, no. I didn't do it for the first time I went there. I-- the trouble was-- the thing was that we went to somebody-- to a very dear friend of my uncle's-- and he told my cousin, who gave out her parents? [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So she listened. This was this woman's son-in-law.

And I said, [NON-ENGLISH], don't tell nobody nothing. You'll do a smart thing not to say. Let them not be afraid of us. We are here back home. We'll get whatever we can what is left. But my cousin was-- poor girl was heartbroken. And she told somebody that I know who, who, who gave out my parents, [INAUDIBLE].

